and speak to cancer survivor and former  good morning america host joan london  and steve alperin the chief executive of  vice platform survivor  net hello to you both thank you very  much indeed for being with us  i want to come on to the potential good  news in  a moment but we've had figures out here  in the uk from cancer research uk  today that some 40 000 fewer people  started cancer treatment in 2020.  cancer hasn't gone away joan by any  stretch has it so how concerning is it  that those diagnoses are being missed  and treatment delayed  you know sarah i was just interviewing a  doctor yesterday who said to me that  they expect this  huge onslaught of  late diagnoses because people are so  reluctant to go into  a medical facility and we know how  important early diagnosis is in breast  cancer  like i had or certainly in skin cancer  in you know if you're not getting your  colonoscopy and your annual skin check  and your annual gynecological checkup  and your mammogram  um you could be putting yourself in  great danger so it's important that  everyone realizes that  these medical centers have been made  safe for us now and you need to go and  have those checkups  yeah and steve um getting a diagnosis at  any time  is incredibly distressing but  particularly  now that the fear is even more  exaggerated what do  users of your platform say and what  advice are you able to give them  thanks for so much for having us on  sarah there are  millions of people who come to us who  are even more afraid  and more anxious about what they might  find  and so what we try to do is take the  information and and decrease that  anxiety just a little bit  um it is safe to go get a mammogram  there are many many tens of thousands of  people every day in each city  going to have these procedures so the  number one thing is go get  checked you don't want to wait if  something's wrong please  go um the people who are doing this  stuff now want to be safe  and it can be done very safely that's  the number one message for prevention  which is still the most powerful tool we  have against cancer  prevention and early screening yeah  really important  messages there joan one of the positives  that has come out of this pandemic  though  is the power of science and when the  global community  comes together we have seen huge  acceleration in scientific breakthroughs  could we see that benefiting the world  of cancer and helping cancer patients  well amazingly a lot of the research  that's been done and  the breakthroughs that we've seen in the  treatment of covid are actually turning  out to be very helpful  in the treatment for cancer but you know  just since my diagnosis which was only  about six years ago  there have been so many advancements  there was no treatment at all for the  kind of  cancer no targeted treatment for triple  negative cancer which i had  and now there are um immunotherapy i  mean that has been the  game changer and it used to be that  you know if a woman had metastatic  breast cancer that was really  a death sentence and we now are learning  um  how to and the goal of course is to make  it a  manageable chronic illness and so  it's really important all the millions  of dollars that have gone  into breast cancer research but i want  to just talk about some of the basics  if you're do yourself exams know your  own  normal so that if anything seems awry  you get that checked right away  you need to be a self-advocate today  whether it's trying to get a  covert vaccination or whether it's  trying to get a second opinion and when  you're facing a life-threatening  disease such as cancer don't be  reluctant to get a second opinion  take somebody with you in for those  first early appointments where  big decisions are made about treatment  women you need to understand what your  breast density  is i didn't know anything about this i  got a clean  3d mammogram the day of my diagnosis and  then i had an ultrasound and found out i  had stage 2  triple negative breast cancer dense  tissue shows up white on a mammogram and  so does cancer  it's very easy to miss a cancer  diagnosis  so if you don't know what your breast  density is and you get mammograms  you call up that radiation clinic that  you went to and ask  if you have very dense tissue because  it's really important to know  you need to know your family history  that's so important  and we still live in a time when a lot  of parents never  really talked about it if they had  cancer they might have been very secret  about it  and you need to kind of get through that  but at the same time  if you never had any family history like  me and you thought well it's not going  to happen to me  only less than 15 percent of women  diagnosed with breast cancer  ever had a family history yeah lots of  questions  to be asked lots of research to be done  and of course  uh checking uh yourself as well steve  we've talked a lot about the mrna  technology  over recent months something many of us  would not have heard of before but is  now  playing a huge part in our lives in the  role out of the fisa vaccine  for example that technology  is there a chance that that could be  used in the fight against cancers  in the future that company with  two i believe turkish emigrates to  germany was originally a cancer company  so i think to put in perspective number  one on world cancer day you have to  acknowledge  the human toll is still extraordinary  and extreme however  we are living through a world historical  moment  in cancer and in medicine in the last  eight years the number of new methods of  of treating disease has doubled before  that for the 100 year history of met  of modern science there might have been  10 or 15 methods in the last 10 years  including  mrna we have doubled that  so if you think about the moment of  these these pathways to attack disease  there is a true industrial revolution  that's not my term there  the people reading it have come with  that term in biotech  and so i think the opportunity for  humanity if you look at 15 of the top  20 pharma companies globally including  some extraordinary uk companies  are betting their financial future i  know somebody who last friday was on a  call  for investors organized by number 10  which the prime minister attended  and it was all about how in the uk can  we take  the extraordinary life science cluster  of academics  and cluster of companies and accelerate  it so  that this moment can be transformed i'll  leave you with one way to look at it  in cancer specifically um in in the u.s  with a president called richard nixon he  declared war on cancer 50 years ago  something like a trillion dollars of  money and 200 medicines mostly  chemotherapies  in the last seven or eight years there  have been 50 to 60 new medicines so the  curve  is like that but it's not good enough  right now what's happening  in in the great universities in the  world in the great pharma companies  they're trying to figure out the next  answers why doesn't immunotherapy work  for some people how can we use mrna  how can we speed up drug development  with ai and so the moment  is extraordinary you see it in in the  public health data in the uk  there's a there's a 20-year period with  a 15 decline in death  in the u.s for the first time in history  treatment  is making a difference for cancer  mortality rather than prevention and so  they  the short form is it's an incredible  positive moment yeah some really  potentially positive news  on the horizon joan i just want to get  your final thoughts  um i know you spend a lot of time  advising and guiding  women as they get a cancer diagnosis  from your own personal experience and to  get a diagnosis now  at a point when fears are exacerbated  fears about  access to treatment fears about catching  covid when your immune system  is compromised but when those support  networks that we would normally rely on  that access to friends and family  are taken away from us what  what can women do and how do you advise  them to get through  the most difficult times well i do think  a support network is really  important especially you know right in  the beginning you put together your  your group of doctors because it's just  not one doctor that treats you  when you're battling cancer and this is  not the time to say i'm type a woman and  i could do this by myself  which i was inclined i was very happy  though that my family said oh no we're  going to be here to support you  but it's also important to make sure  you're getting the right information  and we're living in a time of  just unimaginable disinformation and  misinformation  which can be so dangerous when it comes  to our health that's the reason why i  you know was happy when survivor.net  called me um  steve is the founder of it and because i  knew it was a reliable  platform that was well researched that  you know was caught in constant contact  with doctors  so be very careful where you get your  information at the same time  ask a lot of questions because  everything you just heard from  steve so many new treatments are coming  down the pike when i was diagnosed there  was  a brand new treatment available and i  chose to take it and i think that's  you know one of the reasons why i did so  well so  yeah so it's really been you know we're  not just doing standard of care anymore  they really are doing personalized care  for each person  so that's really a good thing about the  change in  cancer care recently

